<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The `sms` flavour, and the only difference between the two builds.

  ## Why there are two

  Google Play Protect BLOCKS the install of a sideloaded app that requests a
  *restricted permission* — the SMS and Call Log family. Not a warning with an
  "install anyway": on current Android the dialog offers only OK and the app
  does not install. What a person sees is "This app can request access to
  sensitive data. This can increase the risk of identity theft or financial
  fraud."

  `READ_SMS` is the only restricted permission this application asks for, and
  it was making the whole build uninstallable — every other feature included.
  So it lives here rather than in `src/main`, and `standard` is a build that
  goes on a phone.

  Nothing else differs: same application id, same code, same plugin. On the
  `standard` build `SmsInboxPlugin` is still compiled in, Android answers its
  permission check denied because the manifest never asked, and
  `js/core/smsinbox.js` reports DENIED — the path it already had for somebody
  who says no.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        SMS, for Phase 6: reading bank alerts off the device instead of asking
        somebody to paste them one at a time.

        READ THIS BEFORE PUBLISHING. This permission decides where this app can
        be distributed, and the decision was made deliberately rather than by
        adding a line and finding out later.

        READ_SMS is a Play "restricted permission". An app may hold it only if
        it is the user's default SMS handler, or if it has an approved
        Restricted Permission Declaration for a listed use case. FamilyOS is
        not a default SMS handler and does not want to be. A finance app
        reading bank alerts has appeared as a declarable use case, but the
        declarations are reviewed case by case and are routinely refused for
        apps in exactly this position.

        So the honest statement is: **with this line present, this build is for
        sideloading.** That is what the CI debug APK is, and it is how this
        household installs it. Putting this on Play would need a declaration
        that will probably be refused, and if it is refused the answer is to
        remove this permission and use the share-sheet path — not to argue.

        RECEIVE_SMS is deliberately NOT here. It would let the app wake on every
        arriving message, which is passive interception rather than a thing a
        person asks for, and nothing in Phase 6 needs it: the inbox is read when
        somebody opens the screen and asks.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_SMS" />

    <!--
        Same reasoning as GPS: a device or profile with no telephony still runs
        every other part of the application.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.telephony" android:required="false" />
</manifest>
